[Music]  oh  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Music]  [Applause]  okay so i have a lot of interesting news  for you guys i'm so excited to come back  to you with a new sony film simulation  and this time is going to be a high  fidelity copy from fujifilm atari film  simulation first i want to make a short  introduction to the chapters and what  i'll be talking about in this video i'll  start with a comparison between the two  cameras to see the main differences  between fuji and sony next i will reveal  the settings for the profile and how to  set it up in the camera also how to  shoot it in terms of  exposure of temperature and color filter  lastly i'll show you  some raw footage from the camera and how  it was created using the eight  millimeter home film power grade from  davinci and how you can use this slot to  create your own footage also super  interesting i created a sony film  simulation forum on my website so if you  want to find out more details about that  just stick till the end and let's get  started so the sony alternate film  simulation is an amazing profile with  beautiful gradations and colors giving a  great look out of the camera but that  doesn't mean you can upgrade it quite on  the contrary this video is color graded  using the 8 millimeter home fill print  but later in the video i'll show you  some sample footage straight from the  camera so you can make your own decision  whether this profile is for you or not  this profile is about 90 to 95 identical  to the fujifilm with slight differences  to be as accurate as possible  temperature has been set with the help  of a great card on both cameras and shot  at the same time  if we look at the two sample footage  between the color checker first thing  that we notice is the difference in the  contrast sony is based on helgi gamma  and has soft creamy gradations while  fuji has an overall flat look with  crunchy shadows this characteristic will  be very subjective to every person and  neither is better or worse on a sony the  shadows have a slight magenta tint while  fuji has a green one as for color i want  to mention this footage was shot before  final adjustments after comparing the  footage i made some slight modifications  in the color depth to bring them closer  together so actually you will get more  accurate reproduction than in the sample  shown in this video because i was just  too lazy to reshoot the comparison i'll  slowly drag the slider so you can pause  and see for yourself the slight  differences as you can see the blue and  the yellows seem a little bit darker in  this comparison so after checking the  footage i went back into the profile and  brought up the luma values for the  selected colors overall greens are a  little bit more saturated on the sony  and with warmer tones while fuji greens  are more desaturated and leaning towards  cyan or blue sony has more saturated  yellows than the fuji while reds are  more dissaturated and have a tendency  towards orange i feel like sony usually  has warmer color tones compared to other  camera manufacturers so to properly  match the two i recommend shooting 200  to 500 kelvin below normal settings for  example if daylight is usually 5500  kelvin as a rule of thumb we're going to  set the camera between 5300 or 5000  kelvin to bring down the colors towards  the colder tonalities this way you will  shift the greens closer to cyan and red  closer to pink  overall i would say this profile is  about 90 to 95 percent identical to the  fuji which is a really good match  between the two cameras if you ask me  these are really subtle differences and  most people won't even notice at the  first side so now you can match the two  straight out of the camera with minimal  effort  now that we saw how it looks compared to  the fuji let's set it up i'll walk you  through this on sony cameras usually at  the top right corner there's a custom  tube button which is assigned to picture  profiles if not you will have to dig  into the menu and find it on my camera  is at page 12 at the photo icon  so enter the menu and here you can see  all 10 picture profiles if you have a7s3  you will have 11 picture profiles so  you're a lucky one choose one and hit  right to go even deeper into the menus  from here all we have to do is to  replace the settings as shown on the  screen these are the settings for the  sony alternate film simulation picture  profile also with suggested kelvin and  color filter adjustments  remember pressing the menu button will  take you back one page so after you've  finished setting up the profile just hit  ok and half press the shutter to exit  the menu next we will set a kelvin and  color filter i've set this function to  the joystick for fast access for this  video i tested a variation of kelvin  values between four thousand and six  thousand six hundred kelvin and  different color filter options most  preferred was at 4 300 kelvin with color  filter at m2 a3 also another important  detail worth mentioning i always shoot  srgb in favor of adobe rgb  choosing one over another will influence  how the camera renders colors and i  prefer srgb you can change that from the  menu obviously these settings can be  tweaked and you can shoot a different  kelvin or make use of the color filter  to exaggerate different tones and shades  so don't be afraid to play around  pushing the temperature towards 4 500  kelvin will shift the greens which as  you can see results into nice bluish  tones in the grass  like i mentioned before this footage was  color graded to push it even closer to  film look let me show you how it looks  straight out of the camera and how to  use the eight millimeter home film print  to grade your own footage  so this is the image before the power  grade and after the eight millimeter  home fill  this film print is a drag and drop  solution and it comes in multiple  flavors as you can see adjustments are  separated individually and each node is  labeled so it's easier to find the one  that you need and edit or disable it  compared to classical luts power grades  offer you the full control over the  grade which means you can enter each  node to see what i've done and modify if  needed but for this video all i did is  to adjust the contrast and lower the  saturation  you can find this great on my website  now let's check the raw footage this is  straight from the camera and like i said  i was testing the colors so some footage  might look funkier than it should in  some cases i pushed the color filter too  close to magenta and it shifted the  color of the sky towards pink which is  not quite the look i was going for but  when i shot daytime at 4 500 or 5300  kelvin  it rendered very natural and pleasing  colors  i'm very surprised of how well the  greens are looking  dynamic range is amazing with super nice  gradations between highlights mid tones  and shadows  skin tone is perfect and i wouldn't  expect glass from heel g3 gamma  [Music]  compared to s-log helg base iso starts  from 100 or 125 and has very little  noise if you expose them well and  require no grading if you don't plan on  post processing  as for exposure elgi is a hybrid log  gamma and with log in general you want  to expose to the right otherwise it will  be noisier than other profiles for  example cine1 which is probably the best  camera for low light but as long as you  have a fair amount of light i find it  okay to underexpose by half a stop or  even one stop but usually i would expose  at zero exposure or plus one  again it depends on your setup so you  will have to check the image assess and  decide for yourself if it's too noisy or  not okay don't leave yet i have some  exciting news for you just created a  forum on my website where we can all  come together to share custom film  recipes sample photos and just connect  with each other i think being part of a  community is just super cool so i would  like to meet all of you people there why  not join in another reason to join is  that i will be posting all the  experimental film simulation that i'm  currently working on before posting on  youtube and you will be updated of these  profiles before the video is out some of  the film profiles i'm working on at the  moment are classic chrome and kodachrome  64. i already posted some sample  pictures of the kodachrome and this  profile is super good one more thing i  started a hashtag called sony film  simulation so we can all use it every  time we post on social media using these  film profiles so go check the forum and  join in why not you can follow me on  instagram for personal projects or  follow our studio page for some  commercial work and i think that's it  for today  go out experiment have fun and see you  next time

